[{:seon.task/name "avet-seek-allocates"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.db/eid` (one `(d/datoms db :avet a v)` seek, the path of `datahike.db.utils/entid`) costs 13-17 us and 23-32 KB at 100-10k tasks, growing with log n. The work is one seek through 2-3 B-tree nodes of ~6 comparisons each, answering one datom: ~3 us and ~1.3 KB. The cause is an allocation in our Datahike fork (reference-code/datahike: `datahike.db.utils`, `datahike.index`) or our persistent-sorted-set port (reference-code/persistent-sorted-set) on Jolt: find it with seon.profile and `seon.cost/measure`, and fix it there on a jolt/<topic> branch. Correct: the proof green with its :overrun key deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/finding-a-task-by-its-id-is-one-index-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-small-transaction-costs-its-datoms"
  :seon.task/plan "`d/with` of one 5-datom definition record costs 221-233 us and 148-162 KB, flat in program size (100-10k vars). The work is ~15 index inserts at ~1.3 us (`seon.cost/units` :sorted/assoc), 20-30 us: ~10x over. The cause is Datahike's per-transaction overhead on Jolt (tx report, schema checks, index wrappers): find it with seon.profile and fix it in our fork. Correct: the cost proof green at its estimate."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-cost-test/a-definition-record-costs-its-datoms}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-its-attributes"
  :seon.task/plan "The one schema transaction (`seon.db/schema`, 129 attributes, 407 datoms) costs 45-49 ms in memory (`d/db-with` on an empty db; half the schema 15.5-16.7 ms: superlinear), against 0.12 ms for a 2-datom note. Cause, in our Datahike: `update-rschema` (reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:148) recomputes the whole rschema (`dbu/rschema`) after every schema datom, so n attributes cost O(n x attributes). Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike: the rschema derived once per transaction, after its last schema datom (or updated by the one attribute that changed). Estimate: ~407 datom inserts at ~1.3 us plus one rschema derivation over 129 attributes (~50 us), about 1 ms. The schema transaction runs once per store (a check run, the test store), not per boot: it prices every store's creation and any schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-schema-transaction-is-linear-in-its-attributes}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Since task each-id-is-issued-once (worktree session global-ids, 2026-10-23) entity ids and transaction ids come from one page every process shares (seon.db/ids!), reseeded from the heads at open: of what follows, only the roster append remains. Several processes writing different branches of one store is safe and is the design: each commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: `d/branch!` (`datahike.versioning/branch!`) appends the :branches log with `konserve.core/append` (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only (konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token, lmdb.jolt:28). Two processes branching at once can each link after the same last element, and one branch drops out of `d/branches` (its head key stays, so `seon.db/branch?` still finds it); a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. The entity-id counter is per process too: each root reserves its agents' blocks from its own counter (`seon.db/lease-block!`), seeded once past every branch head then committed, so two roots on one store hand out overlapping blocks; that is harmless only while their mains never merge into each other. Production has one root per store, the only process that branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch); several roots on one store (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/root beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) meet both. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or one root per store at a time is a failure point with its edge (`seon.db/open!` refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "deref-keeps-the-connections-config"
  :seon.task/plan "Done, in our Datahike at pin fd0c065 (jolt/deref-config): `datahike.connector/deref-conn` builds the db with the connection's own config, `(dsi/stored->db (assoc stored :config (:config @wrapped-atom)) store)`, as `datahike.connector/-connect-impl*` does, so after a store is moved `(:config @conn)` names its new path and a commit writes the new :store into the head. Before, with a :sync writer it answered the config stored at the head, the path the store was made at. Proof: a moved store's `(get-in @conn [:config :store :path])` is its new path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` is 64 GiB, raised from 1 GiB after two agents died on Datahike's {:type :fatal-commit-error} without confirming MDB_MAP_FULL (a failing commit's cause is now recorded, task seon.agent/a-death-names-its-cause: confirm it); checks and tests delete the branches they made (`seon.db/delete-main!`), and nothing reclaims them. A turn records its basis, not its context text (`:seon.turn/basis`, ~350 bytes, and `:seon.turn/evals`; `seon.context/render` remakes the exact text on that commit). Reproduction: the standing store held 1,305 MB of data.mdb over 278 branches under 68 run mains. test/seon/bench_commit.clj (fresh LMDB stores, bf 64): `d/branch!` writes 3 keys, 382 bytes; a 1-datom commit 11-14 keys, 16.8-27.7 KB; 100 evals committed one by one 4.9-6.9 MB (200-byte values) against 157-546 KB in one commit (26-35x); 50 per-turn commits 16.0 MB with the basis record (24.4 MB at an 8 KB context and 61.3 MB at 32 KB with the context text). `seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes` is red: 20 per-turn commits wrote 1.63 MB against a bound of 0.84 MB (the one commit plus 32 KB of path per commit). Cause: Datahike holds every value inline in its index leaves (twice, :eavt and :aevt), appends go into the tail leaf, and a copy-on-write commit rewrites that whole leaf, so a commit costs about bf/2 x the values already in the tail leaf, not its own size; the remaining gap is values still inline (the reply, reasoning, evals and the eval record's printed value). Branching factor is not the fix (bf 16: 1.5x less). Correct, in order: (1) values over a threshold held out of the index leaves, written once under their own content-addressed key with the datom carrying the reference, in our Datahike fork's node codec so every reader sees plain values (Postgres TOAST; emulated, 50 per-turn commits write ~2.0 MB of index plus each value once); a dependency and format change, root's go first; (2) reclaim: GC keeping only what live branch heads reach, then compaction, as an :io proc when a measured dead/live ratio passes a config key or on demand (DECISIONS: Storage reclaim), through Datahike's and konserve's own mechanisms (reference-code/datahike online-gc), never by deleting a branch, and keeping the commits turns name or past contexts become unreadable; `(datahike.api/gc-storage conn)` on Jolt throws (no dependency provides java.time.Instant), so that class is provided first (Jolt's RFC 0014 `:jolt/provides`) or the GC takes a path without it; (3) the map size set from the measured bound, with that provenance. v1 staged a value over 4,096 bytes out of line (`seon.blob/inline-or-stage`, ~/src/seon)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "dead-readers-are-cleared-at-open"
  :seon.task/plan "LMDB reuses a freed page only when no reader's snapshot is older than the txn that freed it, and a SIGKILLed process's reader slot pins its snapshot until mdb_reader_check clears it; nothing in src calls `konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi/reader-check`. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): four check steps at once, each its own root and agents on one LMDB store, filled the map: mdb_stat showed 65,534 of 65,536 pages used, 60,055 of them free (live data ~85 MB). Correct: clear dead readers where a process opens the env (root boot, agent open: one call), and keep read txns short. The map's size is `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes`; the store's growth is measured by task the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/the-store-grows-with-what-it-keeps"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-dead-readers-slot-does-not-pin-freed-pages}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-commit-names-its-runtime"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: every transaction Seon makes carries the string attribute `:seon.tx/runtime` in its tx-meta (`seon.db/tx-meta`; one ~90 B datom, inline): the writer's group commit (`seon.writer` commit-batch's arg maps), an agent's `seon.agent/transact!` (`dw/apply!`), and `seon.db/open!`'s seed and install; the fork server applies main's tx-meta to its replica (DECISIONS: One store holds the whole system). Its value is `jolt.host/runtime-fingerprint` (jolt/runtime-fingerprint, 8cdbb687): Jolt's own compiled-unit key, `<jolt-version>-<runtime fingerprint>[-tr]` (reference-code/jolt host/chez/loader.ss aot-generation-dir; the fingerprint is baked over a binary's whole image and Chez's boot files, build.ss:2982-3022), which tells two dirty builds apart where `jolt.host/jolt-version` and `jolt.image/runtime-version` do not. Design and measurements: the store-native paper of 2026-10-22 (git history). Replaces nothing; adds one attribute."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-writers-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.procs-test/a-writers-merge-names-its-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/an-agents-transactions-name-their-runtime
                      seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base
                      seon.db-test/a-kept-store-from-before-the-runtime-attribute-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "stored-attributes-take-the-new-words"
  :seon.task/plan "Pass 2 of the rename (owner, 2026-10-22, DECISIONS \"Names are Clojure's\"; AGENTS.md section 7a; pass 1 is seon.doc/names-are-clojures-words), with root's rulings of 2026-10-22 (one word never names two causes):
- stuck: not renamed. Owner, 2026-10-23: \"no automatic retries, send it to root to decide\"; task hold-for-root deletes the refinement chain and with it :seon.task/stuck, seon.reconcile/stuck-tx and the problem class :stuck (a run refused is held for root as my.task/fail! holds a task). seon.reconcile/stuck, which reports every root cause of released work not done (a cancel, a hold), is seon.reconcile/root-causes with rows :seon.reconcile/root-cause and the quiescence report's :root-causes.
- waste stays: \"overrun\" is seon.cost's word for an estimate exceeded, another cause. The owner picks a word (suggested: :seon.run/misdirected, turns spent on what the agent was shown wrongly).
- refusal (not stored): the done rule's reasons, as m/explain's errors are: seon.reconcile/done-refusals -> done-explain, refusals-text -> explain-text, :seon.reconcile/refusal -> :seon.reconcile/not-done with its :refusal key -> :reason; the one error map: seon.contract/refusal -> invalid, seon.config/refusal -> invalid, seon.task's file-refusal -> file-refused, seon.writer's merge-refusal -> merge-refused, seon.schema.datahike's {:refusal ...} -> {:invalid ...}, :seon.print/requery-refusal -> requery-refused; prose says reason, error or refused.
- config keys, stored as facts on main (seon.config/facts): :seon.config.contract/guarantees -> instrument (SEON_INSTRUMENT), :seon.ai.model/thinking-dials -> thinking-modes, :seon.config.root/entrance-port -> server-port (SEON_SERVER_PORT). A checkout's config/local.edn holds the port key, so bin/ports owns its port keys and writes them on every run under their current names, keeping the ports and every other entry; bin/build, bin/test and bin/check run `bin/ports --write` first (their config reads the file; written only when it changes).
- task ids stay: an id is a record (embedded in its runs' ids, branch names and turn bases), so seon.fences, seon.entrance and the tasks named with an old word keep their ids, as branch names do (jolt/land4).
No migration (owner, 2026-10-23: \"why are you migrating 6 facts? isn't it just test data just nuke it and move on\"): the standing store is erased when this merges and its root starts fresh under the new names. Datahike renames no attribute anyway (datahike.schema/find-invalid-schema-updates refuses a :db/ident change; no alias), so a kept store made before this holds its facts under the old names. Rehearsed before the ruling on a copy of next/runs/store: 6 facts on :main (4 thinking-dials, guarantees, entrance-port).
Done: the proof below green; bin/test green; bin/laws, bin/check mg-check, instrument and smoke OK; one full bin/test --all and bin/check 100."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.doc/names-are-clojures-words"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "each-id-is-issued-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: global addressing everywhere, so data is queryable across branches with d/q, d/pull and d/entity (the scratch paper cross-branch-attack-2026-10-23.md (git history)). Entity ids came from per-agent leases of 100,000 (about 5,400 agents per store; a restarted root re-minted a block's unspent tail; two roots on one store overlapped) and transaction ids were per branch ((inc max-tx): two branches forked at one t gave their first transactions one id, measured in docs/storage.md). Now: one page of two 64-bit words beside the store (native/ids.c, seon.db/ids!), mapped by every process (forked children map it with no step), one atomic fetch-add per id (28-34 ns, 200-240 ns at 10 processes, §6); Datahike takes its ids through datahike.ids (jolt/global-ids: set-ids!, with-store-ids; a transaction takes its id when it begins and :max-tx is its branch's last); the page is moved past every head at open. Deleted: seon.db/lease!, lease-block!, lease-spent, lease-use, :seon.config.store/eid-lease, :seon.db/lease-spent and the :unrecorded path, Datahike's eid ceilings, bin/check lease; the fork server's replica replays main's commit as main's transaction (seon.db/replay). docs/storage.md#ids."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/each-id-is-issued-once-across-branches seon.db-test/a-reopened-store-issues-past-every-head seon.procs-test/the-fork-server-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collection-runs-beside-live-agents"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root collects the store only at quiescence with no agent live: `seon.reconcile/quiet` asks once per quiet spell, root's api refuses :collect while an agent is live, and the writer runs `seon.db/collect!` (docs/storage.md#collection). A commit can reuse a value out of line that a concurrent sweep would take, so on a busy root garbage from deleted branches waits for a quiet spell. Correct (the design in docs/storage.md#collection): the mark runs outside LMDB's writer lock; a short delta mark and the sweep run inside one write transaction; a commit that reuses a value re-asserts it in its own transaction. Deletes the api's no-agent-live reason of :collect."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-beside-a-committing-agent-keeps-every-value-it-reuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "values-compress-with-a-trained-dictionary"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every stored value is compressed by the codec `:seon.config.store/compressor` names (zstd level 3, one frame per value, `konserve.jolt.zstd`), each value's header naming its codec (docs/storage.md#compression). Measured there: a dictionary trained on our own values compresses small ones 2-5x better, and faster; it is unused because it needs a corpus that represents the running system. Correct: a dictionary trained (zstd's ZDICT_trainFromBuffer) on a kept store's values, stored in the store under its digest; a value's header names the dictionary, so a store holding values written with and without one reads both; retraining adds a dictionary and never rewrites old values. A konserve fork change on a jolt/<topic> branch, root's go first. Measure bytes per per-turn commit first."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-written-with-a-dictionary-reads-back seon.db-test/a-dictionary-shrinks-what-a-commit-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-deleted-store-leaves-no-semaphore-set"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the robust LMDB build keys each store's SysV semaphore set by ftok(lock.mdb,'M') (mdb.c:6098, low 16 bits of the inode) and removes it only when the last process closes the env holding the exclusive lock (IPC_RMID). Check and test stores are deleted with their set still there. Reproduction: `ipcs -s | grep -c -- --ra-ra-r--` went from 912 to 4,352 in a day; kern.sysv.semmns is 87,381 (about 43k sets). Cause: nothing removes a store's set when the store is deleted. Effects: at the limit every new store's `seon.db/open!` fails in semget, and a key shared with a live store resets that store's writer lock (semctl SETALL). Correct: where a store directory is deleted, read `mti_semid` from its lock file (a konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi helper, a jolt/<topic> change with root's go) and IPC_RMID it. Fewer stores (`seon.check/branches-as-in-production`) slow the growth but do not end it. Deletes: nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-deleted-store-leaves-no-semaphore-set}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-one-eval-commit-costs-what-it-writes"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-24 on jolt/agent-conversation (step 2 D, an agent commits after each eval, task seon.render/a-view-renders-once-per-change-of-its-reads): inside an agent, one model eval of (+ 20 21) with its record costs 137-151k ticks and 0.48-0.54 MB; the seon.agent/commit! after it (Datahike's commit-pending! of that one eval's datoms) 440-449k ticks and 3.19-3.24 MB, the first after the fork 7.06M ticks and 33.3 MB (seon.profile/measure!, three rounds). A commit of a few hundred bytes of datoms allocates about 3 MB: too much for what it writes, though about a millisecond against a model call's 16-18 s (root kept D, 2026-10-24). Cause to find: where a one-eval commit allocates (the tail-leaf copy-on-write this file's store task measured, values inline in the index leaves twice, the commit record and its graph record, the konserve write path, the LZ4/zstd node codec, or the agent's run-ahead apply), by allocation per function (seon.profile/touched) on one commit; then the fix at that owner, in the fork if it is Datahike's. Proof: a cost proof that a commit costs what it writes (bytes allocated within a bound of the datoms' and the rewritten path's size, linear in the datoms, flat in the branch's size), beside seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes, which measures bytes written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-allocates-what-it-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "opening-a-store-costs-what-it-opens"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-27 by the per-function counters (seon.profile/touched, task seon.proofs/tests-are-facts) when seon.proofs-test/a-kept-tree-costs-its-lookup-not-its-program went red at 1.25x its byte estimate: one test-runner/program-db call of a kept tree is 21 contracted calls, 1.49M ticks and 5.57 MB at 10 functions, 1.59M and 5.91 MB at 1,000. Of its bytes, `seon.db/ids!` 1.32 MB, growing to 1.62 MB as the process's program store gains branches (it moves the ids page past every branch head's ids: datahike.writing/set-ids!), so the proof's bytes depend on the branches earlier tests in the shard kept, not on its own input; `seon.db/install-missing!` 1.35 MB a call though nothing is missing (it compares every attribute's definition with the store's); `seon.fx/git-run` 0.93 MB over 5 calls; `seon.db/open-main!` 0.69 MB. Correct: ids! reads the heads' ids once a store is opened, not per branch walked again, or costs the branches changed since; install-missing! costs nothing when the store's schema digest is the program's (one value compared). Proof: the cost proof green with its estimate unchanged, and its bytes the same with 1 and 100 kept branches. Fixed 2026-10-28 (jolt/test-audit): install-missing! reads the db's own (:schema db) rather than d/schema, which rebuilt the schema map on every open; it leaves the top six allocators, and one program-db call is 4.65/4.98/5.34 MB at 10/100/1,000 functions (was 5.60/5.93/6.26 MB in full run 3), 1.07x the estimate. Still open, measured 2026-10-31 from seon.proofs-test/a-process-plans-on-more-scratch-stores-than-its-undo-entries's recorded per-function costs (317 M ticks for 12 fresh stores of a two-file program): seon.db/seed! of a fresh store is 259 M of them, all in install-missing! (24 calls, 260 M self: ~11 M ticks a call), against 4 M for import-tx and 2 M for the reach graph; a fresh store's whole schema installed in two transactions costs ~22 M ticks, which every test with its own store and every check seed pays (task seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all's create, ~22 M, is the same work). Correct: a fresh store costs what a store copy does (seed one, clone it), or the install costs the attributes, not ~11 M a transaction at 1,000 measured alone. It still compares every attribute (no rebuild); the one digest compare is not done. Left: ids! is 1.32/1.62/1.92 MB, about 0.3 MB for every branch the store holds, because datahike.writing/set-ids! reads each branch head's whole record (roster-max) for two numbers. Fixed in Datahike 148ea9a9 (jolt/set-ids-reads-the-heads-not-their-records, pinned on jolt/test-audit): a compact head carries its commit's max-eid and max-tx, so ids! reads each head alone; one program-db call is now 4.19/4.28/4.38 MB at 10/100/1,000 functions and ids! 0.85/0.92/0.98 MB (was 1.32/1.62/1.92). Left: install-missing!'s one digest compare in place of every attribute's."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-roots-heap-costs-what-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Every agent inherits the fork server's heap, so an agent's start heap is root's live heap: 0.97 GB on a fresh store and 1.32 GB on the standing one (355k datoms against 178k; seon.agent at agent.clj:1154 caps it at that plus :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes). The 0.36 GB grows with every recorded test run (106k :seon.proof* datoms) and every write (48k seon.tx and db datoms), with no bound. Measured 2026-10-28 on an APFS clone of runs/store, at main d96168afb1:
(1) The node cache is not the cause. :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes bounds it, but by count: datahike.index.node-cache weighs every node at 4,096 bytes, so the bound is 32,768 nodes. A just-booted standing root fills it (25k misses in 20 s; 12k on a fresh store, so boot reads in proportion to the store, not to what it needs). Evicting all 32,768 nodes and collecting the maximum generation freed 50 MB (1.419 -> 1.367 GB live).
(2) Realized values held outside the cache are the cause. Chez object-counts after a full collection, standing against fresh: vectors 101 MB (1.80 M) against 29 MB (352k), strings 183 MB against 104 MB, pairs 82 MB against 63 MB, bytevectors 27 MB against 18 MB: +180 MB of dynamic objects that a value root holds with the cache empty. Live heap after collection: 1.367 GB against 1.034 GB. The shape (1.45 M more vectors, 170k more strings) fits realized datoms or hiccup.
(3) The index trees hold nothing realized: (:eavt db)'s _root is nil (restored by address on each read), so persistent-sorted-set's Branch _children memo (node-child) retains no tree on the live db.
Not yet known: which of root's values holds (2). compute-size over 'static reaches the whole program (952 MB for every component), so it cannot attribute. The next step is a census by boot phase (object-counts after each seon.root/boot! step) or by holder (the render cache, :results, the task index, derived problem classes), on a clone of runs/store, never on the standing root.
The fix is at the producer that pulls the whole store: it reads what its view needs, through the bounded node cache, and the cache weighs a node by its real size. Owner rule: never pull the whole db."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-cost-test/a-roots-live-heap-is-flat-in-its-recorded-runs}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-idle-root-reads-nothing-from-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-29 (scratch root of main at 200,744 datoms, node cache under a 1 GiB bound so nothing evicts; docs/machine.md#roots-heap): after its boot reads (7,400 nodes, 136 MB weighed) an idle root's cache keeps growing, 1.6 nodes and about 200 hits a second, 0.5 MB every 20 s, with no task, message or commit arriving. Something reads main's indexes on a poll or a render with no input changed (docs/README.md: if nothing has changed, we do nothing). Find the reader (a sampled stack of the root's fibers, or the cache's misses by address and the datoms they hold), fix it at the producer so an idle root's hits and misses stay at zero. Proof: a cost proof that the cache's hits and misses over an idle interval after boot are zero."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-node-cache-is-sized-from-what-an-agent-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "docs/machine.md#roots-heap and :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes: the bound is one key read by the root, the fork server and every agent, and only root's read set is measured (136 MB weighed for main at 200,744 datoms, about 0.7 KB weighed a datom, against a 128 MiB bound). The fork server reads main once for the agents it forks (seon.db/preload!) and an agent inherits that cache and adds its own reads. Measure what agents add on real tasks: an agent's node-cache-stats (hits, misses, weighed bytes) at seon_done over e2e tasks and one real task, and whether main's whole index belongs in the fork server's cache at all. Then size agents' bound from that, with a profile or a role key only if the measurement says they differ."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-held-db-is-a-reference"
  :seon.task/plan "A db value kept past its moment pins its whole version of the index (AGENTS 4, a kept db value is a reference; measured 2026-10-28: the render, vectors and reconciler procs each held ~160 MB, root's *e 1.08 GB), and today each holder works around it by hand (seon.repl/walked-db, \"keep the commit, let the db go\" in seon.writer and seon.db/at-commit). The owner, 2026-10-29: \"maybe we should make the db just be metadata and if you deref it you get the db ... anyone holding or getting a db is just the three metadata keys we need to retrieve it so they can go anywhere\". Make the reference one value: {:branch :commit :t}, plain data (it prints, crosses the wire to an agent, sits in a fact or an error), and one function in seon.db that derefs it through a cache keyed by commit (derived data: evictable, a miss is seon.db/at-commit). Measured 2026-10-29 on the bench (a 40-commit branch): making an old commit's db 0.28 ms and 412 KB, its first query 0.97 ms, the next 0.10 ms. Scope, the owner's choice of the narrow form: references wherever a db is held (process state, caches, errors, proc state, messages to agents, the walk's :walk-db), the ~950 functions and calls that take a db value within one call (456 contracts in 32 files name :seon.host/db) left as they are. Root is an agent on main: its seon.agent/db derefs {:branch :main} (today root's state holds no :db and the call refuses its contract), which replaces seon.repl's agent-only condition on reading an eval's citations. Merges first as its own small task (the reference and its deref, with a cost proof); then the held sites move over in order of measured memory."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-a-few-datoms-writes-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-01 in a test root's agent, 30 commits of one eval record each (seon.agent/commit!, docs/machine.md#an-agents-first-write): a commit of a few datoms allocates 2.3-2.6 MB, flat over the 30 (db->stored 0.36-0.6 MB, datahike.writing/create-commit-id 0.12 MB, konserve.core/multi-assoc 1.7-1.9 MB). Each writes 21-25 values: the leaf and two to three branch levels of each index whose path it changed (current and history, :keep-history? true, v1's ruling) and three records (the commit graph entry, the commit, the branch head); the nodes' contents weigh ~200 KB (datahike.index.node-cache/node-bytes), so the write path allocates ~9x what it writes. Split on one commit's 18 nodes (1.56 MB, ~87 KB a node): konserve.impl.defaults/prepare-multi-assoc's own work 875 KB (each value serialized through a ByteArrayOutputStream, whose buffer doubles, then toByteArray, a copy; the node's write handler), konserve.jolt.zstd/compress 244 KB and konserve.jolt.fasl/encode 231 KB in 36 calls each (half of them a per-value metadata map, encoded and compressed like a node), and the LMDB write 139 KB (konserve.jolt.lmdb's concat-bytes copying header, meta and value into one array, one write transaction per key). Every eval of every agent pays it. The change, in our forks (konserve, then datahike if a part is there), each step measured before and after on the same commit: (1) split prepare-multi-assoc's 875 KB (the buffer and copy against the node handler's data) with seon.profile/counted wraps, as the measurement above did; (2) a value is serialized once into a buffer of its size (fasl's encode-bv, no ByteArrayOutputStream growth and copy); (3) a batch's uniform metadata ({:immutable? true}) encoded once and never compressed (a small map that zstd grows); (4) the LMDB backing writes a batch in one write transaction, header, meta and value put into the reserved value (MDB_RESERVE) without concat-bytes. Estimate: 2.4 MB to under 1.2 MB a commit; a measured number, not this one, goes in the commit. Done 2026-11-01 (konserve 5d89b976, jolt/serialize-once): (2) and (4)'s copy, a commit 2.3-2.6 MB -> 1.74-1.90 MB (seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once); (1) measured: of a batch's 1.08 MB after it, fasl's encoder (konserve.jolt.fasl, jfasl.ss kjc-encode) allocates ~20 KB a node for its ~2.6 KB of bytes (its buffer doubles from 256; every keyword's namespace and name through string->utf8 at every datom: a cache of a keyword's encoded name is the next step), zstd ~13 KB a value, freeze ~9 KB; (3) left: a value's metadata is compressed by the header's one compressor, so not compressing it changes the stored format and its reader. Not changed: what is written (the paths, the history indexes). The other lever, committing per turn rather than per eval, is the run-ahead writer's rule (seon.agent/commit!: when the agent is idle or at a turn's end) and the owner's to change. Proofs: a cost proof of one commit's bytes in seon.db (bytes and contracted calls exact at a fixed input, a commit of 1, 10 and 100 datoms: what the paths cost, never 9x), seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run with its :fixed lowered by what begin-run!'s first commit no longer allocates, and the forks' own gates (a pin bump runs every Jolt and fork gate, AGENTS section 3)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-keeps-one-plain"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: plain data is defined three times and the three disagree: seon.db/plain? (seon.db), seon.contract/kept (seon.contract) and the check Datahike runs on a :db.type/data value (datahike.schema's plain-data?); seon.ref/kept is checked for a fourth; and three ways make a value plain (seon.repl/plainly, seon.contract/bounded, sedn/encode text). The class: a value one definition passes and another refuses (batch 89's datalog Variable records). The change: one Malli schema :seon.db/plain whose predicate is Datahike's own plain-data?, the check the store actually runs; seon.db/plain? and seon.contract/kept are deleted and their callers use :seon.db/plain (m/validate) or the one predicate; seon.ref/kept is folded in if it is the same test. This is the shared interface seon.repl/an-evals-record-has-its-declared-shape and seon.db/a-datom-its-schema-refuses-is-not-stored use, so it merges first, alone. Docs: docs/storage.md states what the store keeps, once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/plain-is-what-the-store-keeps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-datom-its-schema-refuses-is-not-stored"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: every write enters as :seon.db/tx-data [:sequential :any] (resources/seon/schemas/seon.core.edn), the contract of seon.agent/transact!, my.db/transact!, seon.root/transact-main! and seon.repl/record-tx; seon.schema.datahike turns :vector, :sequential, :tuple, :map, :map-of, :any and mixed :or into :db.type/data and never consults the Malli form again, so the store checks only plainness. my.schema/usage-check already validates each stored datom of an attribute against its schema, after the fact. The class: a declared attribute schema that the write does not enforce (batch 89 is one case). The change: at the one point both write paths pass (seon.agent/transact!, in :test and agent mode, and the writer's :tx op), each datom is validated against its attribute's compiled validator; for an attribute derived as :db.type/data the validator is [:and declared :seon.db/plain], derived where the type is derived; a refused datom is the declared error :seon.db/invalid-datom naming the attribute, the path in the value (seon.contract/problems) and the eval or function whose transaction carried it, thrown into the caller's eval as a refused transaction already is. :seon.db/tx-data tightens to ops or maps. Then each attribute gets a real schema in place of [:map] or :any: :seon.turn/error and :seon.end/error (the error schema; :seon.model/failure for the turn's), :seon.process/test-error, :seon.task/cause's :api and :evidence, :seon.merge/key, base, ours and theirs; [:maybe X] under {:optional true} (absence said twice) is cleaned where met. After it, seon.repl/plainly has no job and is deleted. Cost: one validator call per datom; measure the record-write cost proofs before and after and bring the numbers to the owner. Docs: docs/storage.md (the write checks every datom), docs/schema pages it touches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/the-store-keeps-one-plain" "seon.repl/an-evals-record-has-its-declared-shape"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-datom-its-attribute-refuses-is-invalid-and-names-it}}]
